1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:03,880
This robot watches for a smile
before it fully appears.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,570
Then it tries to smile at the same
time as the person in front of it.

3
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:10,040
That takes more than moving
the corners of a mouth.

4
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:14,180
Researchers have been teaching robotic
faces to study their own movements,

5
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:17,360
copy human lips, and predict
an expression as it begins.

6
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,522
And the place to start was a mirror.

7
00:00:20,562 --> 00:00:22,762
At Columbia University, engineers built

8
00:00:22,802 --> 00:00:25,910
a flexible robotic face
with twenty-six motors.

9
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:27,780
That gives the face ways to move.

10
00:00:28,002 --> 00:00:32,282
But the machine still has to learn which
combinations create the shapes it needs.

11
00:00:32,322 --> 00:00:34,220
Think about saying “banana.”

12
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:38,640
Your lips come together, open, stretch,
and move toward the next sound.

13
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:41,562
Now imagine trying to produce
those movements by adjusting

14
00:00:41,602 --> 00:00:44,362
a collection of motors
underneath a rubber face.

15
00:00:44,402 --> 00:00:47,160
You could make its jaw flap
whenever the speaker gets loud.

16
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:49,910
It would look like a very
expensive sock puppet.

17
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:53,135
The researchers wanted
the movements to follow the speech.

18
00:00:53,175 --> 00:00:57,242
So the robot practiced. It moved
its face while watching its reflection,

19
00:00:57,282 --> 00:01:01,082
building a relationship between motor
commands and the appearance they produced.

20
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:03,370
Move these motors, get this mouth shape.

21
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:05,540
Change the combination, get another.

22
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:09,962
That matters because flexible skin moves
differently from a rigid metal part.

23
00:01:10,002 --> 00:01:13,280
Pull at one point, and the material
around it stretches too.

24
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:17,110
The controller has to work
with the face it actually has.

25
00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:19,430
Once it had a way to control
its own appearance,

26
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:22,163
researchers gave it
something else to watch.

27
00:01:22,203 --> 00:01:22,870
People.

28
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:27,030
In the lip-sync study published
in January 2026, the system learned

29
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:31,610
from videos of humans talking and singing,
including hours of YouTube footage.

30
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:34,070
The important information
was the relationship

31
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:36,160
between the sound and the moving mouth.

32
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:39,780
The researchers linked that
to the robot’s model of its own face.

33
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,790
Speech audio could then drive coordinated
lip movements through the motors.

34
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:48,260
The result was a physical face moving
its lips along with speech and song.

35
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:52,287
It could even synchronize with audio
in languages it hadn’t been trained on.

36
00:01:52,327 --> 00:01:55,370
The meaning of those words wasn’t
required for the lip movements.

37
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:57,390
Which gives us a useful distinction:

38
00:01:57,610 --> 00:01:59,910
looking as though you are
speaking and understanding

39
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:02,280
what you are saying
are different problems.

40
00:02:02,510 --> 00:02:04,497
The movements still had flaws.

41
00:02:04,537 --> 00:02:08,140
The team reported difficulties
with sounds like “B” and “W.”

42
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:12,280
A small error is easy to notice
when you’re watching a mouth.

43
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:15,520
But getting the mouth shape
right leaves another problem.

44
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:19,040
When should the face move?
Imagine telling someone good news.

45
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:21,490
They stare at you. You finish smiling.

46
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:24,086
Then their smile switches on.

47
00:02:24,126 --> 00:02:27,470
Even a well-drawn expression can
feel awkward when it arrives late.

48
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:32,610
That timing problem was the focus of the
team’s earlier study, published in 2024.

49
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:35,640
The robot watched videos
of human expressions and learned

50
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:39,320
to recognize small changes
as a smile began to develop.

51
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:41,800
One model predicted the human expression.

52
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:45,310
Another translated the expression
into movements the robot could make.

53
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:48,600
The team reported predicting
an approaching smile about eight hundred

54
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:50,970
and forty milliseconds
ahead of its appearance,

55
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:54,060
allowing the robot to try
to smile alongside the person.

56
00:02:54,290 --> 00:02:56,680
Less than a second of anticipation.

57
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,860
Enough to turn a delayed copy
into a shared-looking moment.

58
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:03,050
These were studies of facial movement.

59
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:06,800
A successful smile doesn’t tell
us whether a machine feels happy.

60
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:10,180
But picture the possible
interaction. You’re speaking.

61
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:11,950
A machine looks toward you.

62
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:16,030
Its mouth follows its voice,
and its expression changes as yours does.

63
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:18,870
Those movements could help
make a tutor easier to follow,

64
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:21,240
or a robotic assistant more approachable.

65
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,940
They also leave us with a question
about how much meaning

66
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:28,050
we attach to a well-timed
response. Watch the smile again.

67
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:32,010
The robot has learned a movement.
You’re the one deciding what it means.
